          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Implementations MUST preserve explicit presence and active <code>oneof</code> members,
          including present zero values. Unsupported schema features MUST return an explicit error
          and never silently drop data. Sharing a <code>.proto</code> schema does not make OmniBuf
          bytes wire-compatible with Protobuf, and libraries MUST document their supported schema
          subset.
        </p>
        <h3 id="sec-11-2">11.2 Conformance Runner &amp; Client Protocol</h3>
        <p>
          Cross-language conformance uses the standalone test runner (
          <code>conformance-runner</code> in{' '}
          <a
            href="https://github.com/omnibuf/omnibuf-go"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="doc-link"
          >
            <code>omnibuf-go</code>
          </a>
          , documented in{' '}
          <a
            href="https://github.com/omnibuf/omnibuf-go/blob/main/CONFORMANCE.md"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="doc-link"
          >
            <code>CONFORMANCE.md</code>
          </a>
          ) paired with a per-language client binary (<code>conformance-go</code>,{' '}
          <code>conformance-es</code>, <code>conformance-rs</code>, <code>conformance-py</code>,{' '}
          <code>conformance-cpp</code>, <code>conformance-nano</code>, <code>conformance-java</code>
          , <code>conformance-cs</code>, <code>conformance-swift</code>,{' '}
          <code>conformance-dart</code>, <code>conformance-php</code>,{' '}
          <code>conformance-elixir</code>). This split follows Protobuf's{' '}
          <code>conformance-test-runner</code> and <code>protovalidate-conformance</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The runner embeds the test corpus and launches the target client as a subprocess. Requests
          arrive on <code>stdin</code> and responses return on <code>stdout</code> as
          length-delimited frames. Every frame starts with a 4-byte little-endian{' '}
          <code>uint32</code> byte length (max 16 MiB) followed by an OmniBuf{' '}
          <code>ConformanceRequest</code> or <code>ConformanceResponse</code>. The client loops on{' '}
          <code>stdin</code> until <code>EOF</code> and logs diagnostics to <code>stderr</code>.
        </p>
        <Code code={sec11ConformanceProtoCode} lang="proto" />
        <p>
          The runner tests four <code>TestTier</code> modes on each vector.{' '}
          <code>TIER_PLUGIN (1)</code> exercises code from <code>protoc-gen-omnibuf-*</code>:
          decoding into dirty structs (verifying omitted fields clear), re-encoding, lazy{' '}
          <code>Opaque</code> getters, and in-place wire splicing (<code>test_opaque_splice</code>{' '}
          and <code>test_opaque_splice_shrink</code>). <code>TIER_PROTOBUF_TYPES (2)</code>{' '}
          exercises standard Protobuf descriptors or generated Protobuf structs with no OmniBuf
          plugin. <code>TIER_ANNOTATED_STRUCT (3)</code> exercises plain language structs, classes,
          or runtime schemas (Go <code>omnibuf</code> struct tags; TypeScript{' '}
          <code>defineMessage</code> or decorators). <code>TIER_BLIND (4)</code> exercises{' '}
          <code>DecodeBlind</code> / <code>decodeBlind</code>, returning{' '}
          <code>WIRE_FORMAT_JSON</code> keyed by Field #0 <code>SchemaMeta</code> names when present
          or <code>field_&lt;N&gt;</code> fallback keys.
        </p>
        <p>
          Map ordering and backpatched Prefix-Varint widths can vary across encoders, so the runner
          decodes the client's returned <code>omnibuf_payload</code> and compares decoded values for
          semantic equality. Requests with <code>self_describing = true</code> must return a valid
          Field #0 <code>SchemaMeta</code> block. On malformed or hostile input vectors,{' '}
          <code>TIER_BLIND</code> MUST return a non-empty <code>parse_error</code>, and every tier
          MUST reject the payload cleanly with no crash, panic, or <code>runtime_error</code>.
          Unimplemented optional tiers or message types MAY return a non-empty <code>skipped</code>{' '}
          string.
        </p>
        <h3 id="sec-11-3">11.3 Conformance Test Coverage</h3>
        <p>
          Generated codecs, descriptor-based codecs, plain-struct codecs, and blind readers MUST
          agree on value encodings wherever their supported features overlap. The conformance suite
          covers:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Every scalar type, prefix width, non-minimal prefix, and field-number boundary.</li>
          <li>
            Every valid array element type, empty arrays, arrays of arrays, arrays of maps, and maps
            containing arrays or maps.
          </li>
          <li>Distinct string/bytes and float/fixed/sfixed interpretations without Field #0.</li>
          <li>
            Recursive message merges, concatenated messages, out-of-order fields, nested repeated
            fields, duplicate map keys, late metadata, present oneof zero values, and size-changing
            wire splicing.
          </li>
          <li>
            Counts near integer limits, mismatched byte lengths, malformed empty collections,
            invalid UTF-8, nesting limits, corrupt Field #0 headers, and unsupported extensions.
          </li>
          <li>
            Protobuf Editions 2023, 2024, and 2026 vectors covering explicit presence zeros
            (including <code>WireBoolFalse</code>, empty strings/bytes, <code>0.0</code>, and enum{' '}
            <code>0</code>), inherited <code>IMPLICIT</code> overrides, open enum unknown values
            (positive and negative), <code>DELIMITED</code> submessages, <code>PACKED</code> and{' '}
            <code>EXPANDED</code> arrays, <code>oneof</code> last-wins resolution, and Field #0
            metadata.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="sec-11-4">11.4 Protobuf Editions Support Matrix</h3>
        <p>
          All 12 language repositories and schema-aware tools target Protobuf Editions{' '}
          <code>2023</code> (<code>1000</code>), <code>2024</code> (<code>1001</code>), and{' '}
          <code>2026</code> (<code>1002</code>), tested against <code>protoc 36.2</code>,{' '}
          <code>buf 1.75.0</code>, and the shared 1109-case conformance suite. Any edition number
          outside <code>1000..1002</code> returns an explicit error.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Supported Editions features:</strong> <code>field_presence</code> (
          <code>EXPLICIT</code> and <code>IMPLICIT</code>, resolved down file, message, oneof, and
          field scopes), open enums (<code>enum_type = OPEN</code> with unknown integers kept intact
          on round-trip), <code>repeated_field_encoding</code> (<code>PACKED</code> and{' '}
          <code>EXPANDED</code>, both encoded as <code>WireArray</code>),{' '}
          <code>message_encoding</code> (<code>LENGTH_PREFIXED</code> and <code>DELIMITED</code>,
          both encoded as <code>WireSubMsg</code> while legacy <code>proto2</code>{' '}
          <code>TYPE_GROUP</code> stays rejected), <code>json_format</code> (<code>ALLOW</code> and{' '}
          <code>LEGACY_BEST_EFFORT</code>), <code>oneof</code> unions, zero defaults, custom option
          extensions under <code>google.protobuf.*Options</code>, and 2024/2026 source or language
          features (<code>export</code>/<code>local</code> visibility, naming/limit checks, Go{' '}
          <code>API_OPAQUE</code>, Java top-level classes, and C# nullable reference types).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rejected everywhere (both codegen and reflection):</strong>{' '}
